**user**:
> I have a variable I want to check in a dplyr filter call. But the name of the variable (a column in a dataframe) is specified by a string variable. How can I do this?
**assistant**:
> In `dplyr`, you can use the `sym()` and `!!` (bang-bang) operators from the `rlang` package to evaluate a string as a column name inside a `filter()` call. Here's a step-by-step guide:
> 
> 1. Make sure you have the `dplyr` and `rlang` libraries loaded.
> 
> 2. Use `sym()` to convert your string variable to a symbol.
> 
> 3. Use `!!` to unquote the symbol inside the `filter()` function.
> 
> Here's an example:
> 
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> library(rlang)
> 
> # Sample dataframe
> df <- data.frame(
>   column1 = c("A", "B", "C"),
>   column2 = c(1, 2, 3)
> )
> 
> # The column name as a string
> column_name <- "column1"
> 
> # Use filter with a dynamic column name
> filtered_df <- df %>%
>   filter(!!sym(column_name) == "A")
> 
> print(filtered_df)
> ```
> 
> In the code above, `sym(column_name)` turns the string `"column1"` into a symbol that can be interpreted as a column name, and `!!` unquotes the symbol so that `filter()` can use it. This way, you can dynamically specify which column to filter on when you run your analysis.
